1795186383395960	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.comment	bn-29o	{"body":"2026-11-20 investigation (no Podman run this time: another project's containers are live on this machine; the three blockers were re-checked with unshare/mount/newuidmap inside playpen). No way found that keeps every guarantee; nothing changed. PRECISE BLOCKERS. (1) uid ranges: the command's user namespace maps exactly one id (uid_map inside reads '1000 0 1'), so there is no range to hand to a container, whoever asks. newuidmap fails twice over: it works through a file capability (cap_setuid=ep), which no_new_privs switches off, and the parent namespace has no second id to map. (2) mounts: Landlock denies every mount(2)/pivot_root to a process in any domain that handles a filesystem right, also inside a new user+mount namespace the process created itself ('unshare -Urm' already fails at 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: EPERM'; with --without-landlock tmpfs and proc mounts work). playpen's rule is a filesystem right (RESOLVE_UNIX), so this cannot be tuned: there is no 'Landlock mode that still permits mounts' on this kernel. (3) packet sockets: the nested user namespace holds nothing over the network namespace; that is the privilege drop, verified before exec. OPTIONS AND COSTS. A. Single-uid containers under --without-landlock (what the trial did; --hide /etc/subuid --hide /etc/subgid makes Podman skip newuidmap): works today; costs the host-socket guarantee outside /run, /tmp, /var/tmp and for sockets created later; images that need several uids fail. B. Map the caller's subuid range into the sandbox: playpen would need newuidmap (a setuid/file-cap helper; spec says none) and the command would need CAP_SETUID in its namespace to map the range onward, which ends 'all capability sets zero'. Weakens two stated guarantees. C. Replace Landlock for such runs by a wider mount layer (walk more roots, hide the home directory's sockets): still blind to sockets created after start; a second-class guard, more code. D. Engine outside, network inside: have Podman join playpen's network namespace. Rootless Podman cannot setns into a namespace owned by playpen's user namespace without running inside it, i.e. with CAP_NET_ADMIN over the filter. Breaks the core guarantee. E. The reverse: a 'playpen guard' that loads and verifies the ruleset in the container's own network namespace from an OCI hook. Keeps Podman whole, but the rules then hold only as long as the container lacks NET_ADMIN/NET_RAW, which Podman's configuration decides, not playpen; no PID-namespace supervision, end time or pass re-read unless rebuilt. A new product surface (size L). F. For blocker 3 alone, independent of the rest and keeps every guarantee: 'run --capture FILE', a packet socket opened by C (which already holds the capability and never runs user code) writing pcap from outside the command. Roughly 150 lines in the most privileged helper plus a size cap; size M. G. Wait for a kernel where Landlock lets a domain mount inside its own mount namespace; nothing to use today. Owner decision needed: whether container use is worth E (or A as documented, weaker mode); F can be built on its own."}	blake3:e2ZDbDNtPN0oQZwCjWS4Jv39Fk9-11ex2MVe8rhvkGc
1795186693062388	sippycup-2u2m2v	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-7sw	{"description":"Found in the 2026-11-20 review. check_stdio let connected TCP through as 'reaches only its peer'. connect(AF_UNSPEC) dissolves the connection and the socket, which stays in the host's network namespace, can then connect to any address the host reaches (shown from a --no-network sandbox to a second listener on the host's loopback). The same for a Unix datagram or unconnected stream socket on stdio under --without-landlock, which reaches the host's abstract sockets. Fix: every IP socket on stdio is refused; without Landlock a Unix socket only if it is a connected stream/seqpacket or a listener.","kind":"bug","title":"A connected TCP socket on standard output can be re-pointed past the filter"}	blake3:ZFoSGAxnMqMwtiU3EGxVBSQjrc81mLzI-KTZdI-KAv0
